  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1107 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAnother dry and warm day is in store for Friday, before the\nremnants of Hurricane Isaias lift through the region on Saturday.\nWhile the remnant surface low will likely track near the MS/AL\nborder, an expansive wind field will rotate around north and west\nthe surface low, affecting areas as far away as northeast AR.\nThis wind will likely be the main impact for the Midsouth. NBM\nensemble mean sustained winds appear reasonable, averaging 20KT\nSaturday morning. Gusts will be the primary concern, with a band\nof 925mb winds near 50KT over north MS mid-morning Saturday. 35KT\n(40mph) appears to be a good gust potential for much of Midsouth,\neast of the MS River. Briefly stronger transient gusts cannot be\nruled out ahead of any heavy rain bands over north MS Saturday\nmorning. A Wind Advisory will likely be issued later\ntoday/Friday.\n\nQPF remains largely unchanged from earlier forecasts. Isaias will\nmove relatively quickly through the region. Despite very dry\nsoils, rain rates may exceed the ability for absorption. A Flood\nWatch remains in effect for a good portion of the Midsouth east\nof the MS River.\n\nAs Isaias exits early Sunday, a midlevel ridge will move into the\nlower MS River Valley. The ridge will amplify to 592dam at 500mb\nover central MS, and promises to bring a return of late summer-\nlike high temperatures as we approach mid-October.",
